 ## Operating characteristics
--   **Time Complexity:** The trajectory computation is $O(N_b \cdot N_n)$ where $N_b$ is the number of beams and $N_n$ is the number of nodes per beam. The radiance estimation is $O(N_b \cdot N_q \cdot k)$ where $N_q$ is the number of query points per beam and $k$ is the number of contributing photons per query. Finding $k$ photons depends on the spatial indexing of the photon map (e.g., $O(\log N + k)$ with a k-d tree).
--   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_n \cdot N_b)$ for storing beam nodes and transmittance values, plus $O(N_p)$ for the photon map.
--   **Strengths:** Accurately handles light transport in continuously refractive media where paths are curved. Avoids the artifacts of straight-line approximations in heterogeneous refractive media.
--   **Limitations:** Requires numerical integration (RK4) which is more expensive than straight-line tracing. The $n^{-2}$ term integration adds complexity. Performance depends on the number of beam nodes and nearby photons.
--   **When to use:** When rendering scenes with participating media that have spatially varying refractive indices causing significant light bending.
--   **When not to use:** For homogeneous media or media with negligible refractive index variation, where straight-line beam estimation is sufficient and faster.
